---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { allTools, getToolName } from '../data/tools';
import { disabledPersistenceSlugs } from '../data/persistence';
import { aboutNetworkToolSlugs, aboutNetworkNoteKey } from '../data/network';
import { t } from '../i18n/utils';

const sensitiveTools = allTools.filter((tool) => disabledPersistenceSlugs.includes(tool.slug));
const networkTools = allTools.filter((tool) => aboutNetworkToolSlugs.includes(tool.slug));
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About ZeroTool — Free Developer Tools for Everyone"
  description="Learn about ZeroTool: free developer tools that run in your browser. No sign-up, and the data you process stays on your device. See which analytics and ads the site uses."
  lang="en"
>
  <div class="prose-container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About ZeroTool</h1>
      <p class="prose-meta">Built for developers, by developers.</p>

      <h2>Our Mission</h2>
      <p>
        ZeroTool is a growing collection of free, privacy-first developer tools for the modern web.
        We believe that essential developer utilities should be accessible to everyone — regardless
        of budget, location, or account status. Every tool on ZeroTool is free to use, requires no
        sign-up, and works directly in your browser.
      </p>

      <h2>How It Works</h2>
      <p>
        The tools process the text, files and keys you give them with JavaScript in your browser
        tab and do not upload them. When you paste a JSON document, encode a string to Base64, or
        generate a UUID, the work happens inside your browser tab. A few tools send requests because
        of what they do; they are listed under What Stays on Your Device below.
      </p>
      <p>
        This design is intentional. Developers often work with sensitive data — API responses,
        credentials, private keys, internal configuration files. We wanted to build tools you
        could trust with that data, and the simplest way to earn that trust is to never touch
        the data at all.
      </p>

      <h2>What We Build</h2>
      <p>
        ZeroTool covers a broad range of day-to-day developer needs:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Data tools</strong> — JSON formatter, diff viewer, CSV converter</li>
        <li><strong>Encoding tools</strong> — Base64, URL encode/decode, HTML entities</li>
        <li><strong>Text utilities</strong> — case converter, lorem ipsum, word counter</li>
        <li><strong>Security tools</strong> — hash generators (MD5, SHA-256), password generators</li>
        <li><strong>Developer utilities</strong> — UUID generator, regex tester, color picker</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We ship new tools regularly based on community requests and our own development needs.
        If a tool is missing, let us know.
      </p>

      <h2>What Stays on Your Device</h2>
      <p>
        The text, files, keys, and other input you give a tool are processed by JavaScript in your
        browser tab. ZeroTool does not upload them and has no server that could receive them. The
        results stay in your browser too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some tools save your latest input or settings in your browser's <code>localStorage</code>, so a
        page reload does not lose your work. That data stays on your device; the
        <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> lists what is saved. Tools that handle credentials, keys,
        tokens, or private files save nothing.
      </p>
      <p>Some tools use the network because of what they do:</p>
      <ul>
        {networkTools.map((tool) => (
          <li><a href={`/tools/${tool.slug}/`}>{getToolName(tool, 'en')}</a> — {t('en', aboutNetworkNoteKey(tool.slug))}.</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Analytics and Ads</h2>
      <p>ZeroTool is free because it shows ads. Most pages load two Google services:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Google Analytics 4</strong> — counts page views and tool use. A tool-use event
          records the tool name and the action you ran (for example <code>copy</code>), not your input.</li>
        <li><strong>Google AdSense</strong> — shows ads. Google can set cookies to serve and measure
          ads, under Google's own policies.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Pages for tools that handle credentials, keys, tokens, or private files and text load neither
        Google Analytics nor AdSense:
        {sensitiveTools.map((tool, i) => (
          <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={`/tools/${tool.slug}/`}>{getToolName(tool, 'en')}</a></>
        ))}.
      </p>

      <h2>Open Source</h2>
      <p>
        The source code of ZeroTool is public on <a href="https://github.com/kassol/zerotool-dev">GitHub</a>. You can read how each tool
        works and check the statements on this page against the code. Bug reports and tool requests
        are welcome in <a href="https://github.com/kassol/zerotool-dev/issues">GitHub Issues</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Get in Touch</h2>
      <p>
        We're a small, independent team. If you have a tool request, found a bug, or just want
        to say hello, reach out at <a href="mailto:hello@zerotool.dev">hello@zerotool.dev</a>.
        We read every message.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
